/-!
# The object package with a declared datatype, read in the domain

The object package with its numbers is read in the domain by `objectChurchReading`, which
validates it (`objectChurchReading_valid`). This module reads the object package with any simple
datatype admissible over it (`dataChurch d`, for `d.Admissible objectChurch`): a type, constructors
whose fields are the datatype itself or closed types of the object package, and a recursor with
one computation rule for each constructor. The reading `dataReading d` validates the package and
agrees with the object reading on every constant of the object package (`dataReading_valid`,
`objectChurch_data_reading`).

**The reading.** The datatype denotes the declared datatype of the domain at its parameters, the
closed field types of its constructors in order, read by the object reading (`dataTypeI`,
`dataParams`). A constructor's kind records the datatype and the shape of each field: the datatype
itself, or the next parameter (`fieldShapes`, `ctorShapes`). Each new constant denotes a Church
constant, a function projected onto the denotation of its declared type:

* a constructor denotes the constructor element of its arguments, curried (`curryI`) and projected
  onto the nondependent function type of its fields (`ctorConstI`, `cinterp_ctorType`), so at
  elements of its field types it is the constructor element (`appSpine_ctorConstI`);
* the recursor denotes recursion over the datatype by approximation (`Ideal.recI`), with one method
  for each constructor (`methodsAt`): at a constructor, its method applied to the fields and to the
  recursive values, each read at the motive at its field (`caseValue`, `caseValue_eval`); curried
  over the motive, the methods and the element, and projected onto the denotation of its declared
  type (`recConstI`, `dataReading_recursor_eq`).

**Why it is valid.** Universes, heads and head equality are read as in the object package. The
object package's constants keep their values, and their declared types mention only its own
constants. The type of the datatype is generated by type tokens, since its parameters are types of
the object package (`typeGenerated_dataTypeI`); the constructors and the recursor are Church
constants (`dataReading_constants`). A root step of the object package is valid because its schema
mentions only the object package's constants, so its spine facts carry over to the object reading
(`dataReading_objectRoot`). The computation rule of the recursor at a constructor is valid
(`dataReading_iota`): the spine facts of its left side make the constructor's arguments elements of
their field types, recursion computes at the constructor element by the constructor's method
(`Ideal.recI_ctorI`, `recursor_at_ctor`), and the recursor at a recursive field is recursion there,
read at the motive (`appSpine_recursor`).

**The three faces.** This is the typed judgment's face read in the domain. The extensional face of
the same package is its set model (`objectDeclarations_model`), where the datatype is the least set
closed under its constructors; the operational face is the recursor's computation rules, whose
validity in the reading is shown here.

**The lists of numbers** are an instance (`listDecl`: `nil`, `cons : num → list → list`,
`list-rec`); the binary trees of numbers are another (`ObjectTrees`). Positive: the length of the
one-element list of the numeral one denotes one (`lengthOf_one_value`). Negative: reading `cons`
as the constant function to the empty list keeps it an element of its declared type but breaks the
recursor's rule at `cons`, so that reading is not valid (`badCons_not_valid`). The reading
separates the constructors of every admissible datatype (`appSpine_ctorConstI_proj`,
`Ideal.ctorI_ne_of_ne`); with the typing of their arguments this gives that no closed equality
of two different constructors is derivable (`ConstructorView`, `ctor_not_equal_ctor`).
-/

set_option autoImplicit false

